<article class="env-lemma" id="019O">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/019O">Lemma <span data-tag="019O">14.26.8</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category with finite coproducts. Let $U$ be a simplicial object of $\mathcal{C}$. Consider the maps $e_1, e_0 : U \to U \times \Delta [1]$, and $\pi : U \times \Delta [1] \to U$, see Lemma <a href="/tag/017E" data-tag="017E">14.13.3</a>. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We have $\pi \circ e_1 = \pi \circ e_0 = \text{id}_ U$, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The morphisms $\text{id}_{U \times \Delta [1]}$, and $e_0 \circ \pi $ are homotopic. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The morphisms $\text{id}_{U \times \Delta [1]}$, and $e_1 \circ \pi $ are homotopic. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The first assertion is trivial. Consider the map of simplicial sets $\Delta [1] \times \Delta [1] \longrightarrow \Delta [1]$ which in degree $n$ assigns to a pair $(\beta _1, \beta _2)$, $\beta _ i : [n] \to [1]$ the morphism $\beta : [n] \to [1]$ defined by the rule 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \beta (i) = \max \{ \beta _1(i), \beta _2(i)\} .  \]
</div>
    <p>
       It is a morphism of simplicial sets, because the action $\Delta [1](\varphi ) : \Delta [1]_ n \to \Delta [1]_ m$ of $\varphi : [m] \to [n]$ is by precomposing. Using the notation $\Delta [1]_ n = \{ \alpha ^ n_0, \ldots , \alpha ^ n_{n + 1}\} $ introduced above, we have $\beta = \alpha ^ n_0$ if $\beta _2 = \alpha ^ n_0$ and $\beta = \beta _1$ if $\beta _2 = \alpha ^ n_{n + 1}$. Since $\alpha ^ n_0$, resp. $\alpha ^ n_{n + 1}$ is the constant map with value $1$, resp. $0$, this implies that the induced morphism 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  U \times \Delta [1] \times \Delta [1] \longrightarrow U \times \Delta [1]  \]
</div>
    <p>
       of Lemma <a href="/tag/017E" data-tag="017E">14.13.3</a> is a homotopy from $\text{id}_{U \times \Delta [1]}$ to $e_1 \circ \pi $. Similarly for $e_0 \circ \pi $ (use minimum instead of maximum). 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>